WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.633
Reliable failure behavior is part of HTTP client security.

2
00:00:03.633 --> 00:00:11.303
A client without timeouts can tie up threads, sockets, memory, and user workflows while waiting for a remote system.

3
00:00:11.303 --> 00:00:16.148
Set connection timeouts, read timeouts, and where appropriate, a total operation deadline.

4
00:00:16.148 --> 00:00:20.992
Cancellation should be available when the caller no longer needs the result.

5
00:00:20.992 --> 00:00:22.203
Retries need policy.

6
00:00:22.203 --> 00:00:25.029
Retrying a safe lookup may improve resilience.

7
00:00:25.029 --> 00:00:31.892
Retrying a non-idempotent operation can duplicate effects, increase load, or make the failure harder to understand.

8
00:00:31.892 --> 00:00:39.562
Retry rules should consider method semantics, request body replay, remote service guidance, backoff, jitter, maximum attempts, and whether a

9
00:00:39.562 --> 00:00:43.599
circuit-breaker style control is needed at a higher layer.

10
00:00:43.599 --> 00:00:46.425
Error classification helps both security and operations.

11
00:00:46.425 --> 00:00:53.287
DNS failure, TLS validation failure, authentication failure, timeout, protocol error, response validation failure, and application-level rejection

12
00:00:53.287 --> 00:00:56.517
should not all collapse into one vague result.

13
00:00:56.517 --> 00:01:02.976
The caller needs enough information to choose a safe response without exposing internal details or secrets.

14
00:01:02.976 --> 00:01:05.398
Observability should record useful, safe context.

15
00:01:05.398 --> 00:01:12.665
Endpoint labels, status categories, timing, retry counts, validation outcomes, correlation identifiers, and sanitized error classes can help teams

16
00:01:12.665 --> 00:01:13.068
troubleshoot.

17
00:01:13.068 --> 00:01:21.546
Authorization headers, cookies, tokens, request bodies with sensitive data, private keys, and sensitive response fields should stay out of logs and

18
00:01:21.546 --> 00:01:21.950
traces.

19
00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:24.372
A well-designed client fails predictably.

20
00:01:24.372 --> 00:01:33.253
It stops when trust checks fail, limits resource use, avoids uncontrolled retry storms, and gives maintainers enough safe evidence to understand what

21
00:01:33.253 --> 00:01:33.657
happened.

22
00:01:33.657 --> 00:01:42.134
That evidence should support dashboards, alerts, and incident review without turning logs into a secondary store of sensitive request or response

23
00:01:42.134 --> 00:01:44.960
data or credentials for later exposure risk.
